   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "task-4-analysis-md",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4.4 In-Depth Error Analysis Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 1. Technical Section Differences (Hardest vs. Easiest)\n",
    "- **Easiest Sections \u2014 Section B (Transporting; Operations, $r = 0.8973$) & Section F (Mechanical Engineering, $r = 0.8521$):**\n",
    "  Mechanical and operational patents predominantly use physical, standardized engineering terms (*bracket*, *hydraulic cylinder*, *bearing*, *valve*). These concrete physical concepts map clearly into the model's pretrained representations, leading to high correlation and low variance.\n",
    "- **Hardest Sections \u2014 Section G (Physics, $r = 0.7066$) & Section C (Chemistry; Metallurgy, $r = 0.7790$):**\n",
    "  Physics and chemistry patents feature highly abstract terminology, mathematical/optical definitions, and complex IUPAC chemical nomenclature (e.g. *cochineal* vs. *azo dye*). Subword tokenizers fragment these specialized terms into generic subword units, making fine-grained semantic similarity harder to predict.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Section | CPC Domain Name | Validation Count | Pearson Correlation ($r$) | Relative Difficulty |\n",
    "| :---: | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n",
    "| **B** | Performing operations; transporting | 1,926 | **0.8973** | Easiest |\n",
    "| **F** | Mechanical engineering; lighting; heating | 968 | **0.8521** | Very Easy |\n",
    "| **H** | Electricity | 933 | **0.8004** | Moderate |\n",
    "| **E** | Fixed constructions | 373 | **0.7986** | Moderate |\n",
    "| **D** | Textiles; paper | 199 | **0.7951** | Moderate |\n",
    "| **A** | Human necessities | 943 | **0.7823** | Difficult |\n",
    "| **C** | Chemistry; metallurgy | 1,177 | **0.7790** | Difficult |\n",
    "| **G** | Physics | 1,040 | **0.7066** | Hardest |\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Label Imbalance and Regression Score Calibration\n",
    "\n",
    "| True Score | Validation Count | Share of Data | Mean Prediction | Std Dev | Calibration Note |\n",
    "| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n",
    "| **0.00** | 1,522 | 20.1% | **0.1128** | 0.1552 | Moderate upward shrinkage toward mean (avoids hard 0) |\n",
    "| **0.25** | 2,465 | 32.6% | **0.3041** | 0.1196 | Well centered near 0.25\u20130.30 boundary |\n",
    "| **0.50** | 2,475 | 32.7% | **0.4901** | 0.1437 | Near-perfect alignment with true score 0.50 |\n",
    "| **0.75** | 832 | 11.0% | **0.6640** | 0.1636 | Conservative downward shrinkage toward mean |\n",
    "| **1.00** | 265 | 3.5% | **1.0136** | 0.1247 | Strong confidence on identical synonyms |\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Connection to Label Imbalance:** Intermediate scores (0.25 and 0.50) make up **>65%** of the entire dataset, while identical matches (1.00) represent only **3.5%**. The model naturally centers its variance around 0.30\u20130.50. Despite this, the mean predictions show excellent monotonic calibration: $0.11 \\rightarrow 0.30 \\rightarrow 0.49 \\rightarrow 0.66 \\rightarrow 1.01$.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 3. Classification of the Top 10 Errors into Failure Types\n",
    "\n",
    "Inspection of the top 10 absolute errors from validation output reveals four key error patterns:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Word-Order Inversion ($|\\Delta| = 0.9316$):**\n",
    "   - *Example:* Anchor *\"punch face\"* vs. Target *\"face punch\"* (True = 0.00; Pred = 0.9316).\n",
    "   - *Root Cause:* Self-attention observes identical bag-of-words tokens. Because *\"punch\"* and *\"face\"* appear in both, the cross-encoder predicts almost identical similarity, missing the functional semantic inversion between a tool's striking surface and an assault action.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Ground Truth Annotation Ambiguity / Syntactic Variants ($|\\Delta| \\approx 0.73\u20130.82$):**\n",
    "   - *Example 1:* *\"boom hydraulic cylinder\"* vs. *\"boom a hydraulic cylinder\"* (True = 0.00; Pred = 0.8228).\n",
    "   - *Example 2:* *\"imaging axis\"* vs. *\"imaging of axis\"* / *\"image of axis\"* (True = 0.00; Pred = 0.7920 and 0.7334).\n",
    "   - *Root Cause:* These pairs differ only by an article (*\"a\"*) or preposition (*\"of\"*). Semantically, both refer to the exact same physical patent apparatus. Human annotators marked them 0.00 (likely due to strict phrase parsing rules or annotator disagreement), leading to massive false positive errors by any semantic model.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Domain-Specific Acronyms & Terminology (False Negatives, $|\\Delta| = 0.6958$):**\n",
    "   - *Example:* Anchor *\"end pins\"* vs. Target *\"wood pin\"* (True = 0.75; Pred = 0.0542).\n",
    "   - *Root Cause:* In musical instrument patents (Section G10), an *end pin* is made of wood/metal to hold strings or support a cello. Without domain-specific knowledge of lutherie, general language models assume *end* and *wood* have zero relation.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Fine Chemical Distinction ($|\\Delta| = 0.6406$):**\n",
    "   - *Example:* Anchor *\"cochineal\"* vs. Target *\"azo dye\"* (Section C13, True = 0.00; Pred = 0.6406).\n",
    "   - *Root Cause:* Both are well-known red colorants (cochineal is a natural insect-derived carmine pigment; azo dye is a synthetic petroleum-derived colorant). The model recognizes both as dyes and predicts high similarity (~0.64), whereas patent examiners require exact chemical structural matching (True = 0.00).\n"
   ]
  },
